{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And you shall bring a new meal-offering to HaShem.</b> This is a sign for the day of the giving of the Torah, for the Torah must be new to a person every day, as though he received it this day from Mount Sinai. And the reason that the Torah does not explicitly mention that this day is the day of the giving of the Torah — and likewise the Torah does not explicitly mention that Rosh HaShanah is a day of judgment — is one and the same reason, as I wrote in my work עָלְלוֹת אֶפְרַיִם: concerning the giving of the Torah, HaShem did not wish to fix a specific day, because a person must regard it every single day of the year as though on that very day he received it from Mount Sinai. For indeed our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Eruvin 54b) that the Torah is compared to a fig: just as the infant keeps finding a new taste in it as long as he gnaws at it, so too the Torah — everyone who engages with it finds a new taste in it every day. Therefore it is fitting that it should seem to him every day as though he received it this day from Mount Sinai. And if so, every day is the giving of the Torah for those who engage with it — therefore it is not proper to fix a specific day for its giving. And therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sifrei Va'etchanan 6): 'Let the words of Torah be new upon you,' and not like an old thing at which a person's heart grows weary — for in truth you find in it something new every single day. And therefore the day of its giving is not explicitly stated in the Torah beyond what is alluded to in the bringing of a new meal-offering, to teach that the Torah is a new meal-offering every single day." "And the two loaves were leavened bread — leaven being that by which the evil inclination is likened, for in the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Berakhot 17a) it is called 'the leaven in the dough,' because in the place where the Torah is found, the evil inclination cannot do harm, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Kiddushin 30b): the Holy One, blessed be He, said: 'I created the evil inclination; I created for it its spice' — that is, the Torah. And were it not for the evil inclination, the Holy One, blessed be He, would not have brought the Torah down from the higher ones to the lower ones, for with this argument Moses our Master defeated the angels, when they said before the Holy One, blessed be He: 'A treasured possession do You have… and You wish to give it to flesh and blood!' And Moses answered them: 'Is there an evil inclination among you…?' (Shabbat 88b–89a) — meaning that there was a need to give the Torah to the lower ones in order to give them a spice against the evil inclination that is bound up within them, to supply what they lack. Therefore it says: 'they shall be baked leavened.'" "And this reason also conceals the day of judgment of Rosh HaShanah, so that a person would not follow the stubbornness of his heart and accustom himself to sinning all the days of the year, planning to mend his deeds only when he draws near to the day of HaShem upon which He sits upon the throne of judgment — but rather he should imagine in his soul that every single day HaShem sits upon His throne for judgment and inspects his ledger, and through this he will be in repentance every single day. And so too there is one who says: a person is judged every single day (Rosh HaShanah 16a), as it says (Job 7:18): 'וְתִפְקְדֶנּוּ לִבְקָרִים לִרְגָעִים תִּבְחָנֶנּוּ.' And in the aforementioned work of mine I elaborated on these two reasons, for they are sound to the discerning mind."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:08.441649437Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}